+   b. Retrieve points: Get intersection points and normals into `reference_V` [S1, unofficial].
+   c. Handle empty cells: If `reference_V` is empty (length 0), skip the cell [S1, unofficial].
+   d. Construct linear system: $A = reference\_V[:, 3:6]$, $b = reference\_V[:, 3:6] \times reference\_V[:, 0:3]$ (element-wise multiplication of normals and positions) [S1, unofficial].
+   e. Regularization: Initialize `reg_scale = 1e-6`. Calculate `reg_pos` as the mean position of intersection points. Add regularization rows to $A$ and $b$ to avoid singular matrices [S1, unofficial].
+   f. Solve least squares: $x = \text{lstsq}(A, b)[0]$ [S1, unofficial].
+   g. Boundary adjustment: If any component of $x$ is outside $[0, 1]$, enter an adjustment loop. Initialize `tmp_scale = 1`. While $x$ is out of bounds: increase `reg_scale` by `tmp_scale`, recompute $A$ and $b$, solve for $x$. If `reg_scale > 1000`, break and reset `reg_scale = 1e-6`. If still out of bounds, decrease `reg_scale` by `tmp_scale / 2` in a loop of 10 iterations, adjusting `reg_scale` and solving again. If still out of bounds after the loop, perform one final solve with the current `reg_scale` [S1, unofficial].
+   h. Store result: Store $x$ into `LOD_gt_float[i, j, k]` [S1, unofficial].
+9. **Save Data:** Save all generated arrays to an HDF5 file with the specified dataset names and shapes [S1, unofficial].
 
